                <h2>This Week's Observation: Account Management Issues Are Surfacing</h2>
<p>This week, while communicating with several overseas marketing teams, we noticed that agency account setup problems are coming to a head. One performance marketing lead complained: "We're running Meta, Google, and TikTok simultaneously, each with different agencies. Just keeping track of account statuses means flipping through seven or eight group chats."</p>
<p>This isn't an isolated case. Yiyu Digital Media observed that many cross-border e-commerce and app teams still rely on "spreadsheets + group chats" for agency account management—recording account info in Excel, confirming deposits and approvals via WeChat or Lark groups. This works when managing a handful of accounts but breaks down as operations scale to multi-platform, multi-agency, multi-account complexity.</p>
<h2>Transparent Tracking: From "Manual Monitoring" to "At a Glance"</h2>
<p>The biggest problem with traditional approaches is <strong>information opacity</strong>. When an agency account setup involves client operations, agency sales, platform review, and finance teams, every information handoff risks delays or gaps.</p>
<p>One tool app's performance team experienced this scenario: the agency confirmed the account was live, but the operations team discovered three days later that the deposit never arrived, delaying that week's campaign. The issue wasn't anyone's fault—it's that the entire process lacked visual milestone tracking.</p>
<p>The core value of systematic kanban boards is <strong>making processes transparent</strong>. Through kanban views, teams can clearly see the complete chain for each account: application submission, credential review, agency coordination, approval confirmation, and deposit arrival. Each milestone marks the responsible party, expected completion time, and current status—all participants can sync in real-time.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Reducing repetitive confirmations means conserving your operations team's energy.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Proactive Risk Control: Avoiding "Emergency Pauses"</h2>
<p>Agency account setup isn't just an efficiency issue—it's <strong>the front line of risk management</strong>.</p>
<p>This week we noticed teams forced to interrupt campaigns because an agency account became anomalous or deposits were delayed. These passive situations usually stem from lacking systematic early-warning mechanisms—problems are discovered after they happen rather than anticipated before.</p>
<p>Systematic kanban boards can preset milestone reminder rules:</p>
<ul>
<li>Deposit initiated but not confirmed within 48 hours → auto-flag warning</li>
<li>Approval process stalled beyond 5 business days → trigger escalation</li>
<li>Single account balance below preset threshold → prioritize deposit reminder</li>
</ul>
<p>These rules seem simple, but they shift risk control from "firefighting after the fact" to "prevention beforehand."</p>
<h2>Reconciliation Efficiency: Unified Views Reduce "Manual Aggregation"</h2>
<p>Financial reconciliation is another frequent pain point in agency account management.</p>
<p>One cross-border e-commerce team spent 2-3 days monthly organizing consumption reports, deposit records, and balance data across agency accounts. Different platforms have inconsistent statement formats, manual aggregation invites errors, and "what doesn't match up" becomes the biggest headache for operations and finance.</p>
<p>Kanban board multi-dimensional views aggregate scattered consumption data, deposit records, and account balances into a unified interface. Operations teams can quickly locate anomalous data on the kanban; finance reconciliation cycles shrink from three days to half a day.</p>
<h2>Practical Recommendations: Three Steps to Build Your Account Setup Kanban</h2>
<p>If your team currently uses spreadsheets for agency account management, start systematic transformation with these three steps:</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Map All Process Milestones</strong><br />
   List every required step from account application submission to deposit confirmation, clarifying responsible parties and standard timelines for each. This is the foundation for kanban setup.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Set Key Milestones and Alert Rules</strong><br />
   Determine which milestones need automatic reminders—deposit confirmations, approval notifications, balance alerts. Kanban tools (Lark Multidimensional Tables, Notion, Trello) can all implement these features.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Regular Reviews and Optimization</strong><br />
   After launching the kanban, hold weekly reviews: Which milestones frequently bottleneck? Which rules need adjustment? Use data to drive continuous process improvement.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Conclusion: Every Visualization Step Accumulates into Efficiency</h2>
<p>Efficiency gaps in agency account setup often hide in "unseen" communication costs and waiting times. The value of systematic kanban boards isn't simply introducing a tool—it's switching teams from "person-to-person" passive management to "data-driven" proactive management.</p>
<p>For overseas marketing teams, agency account setup efficiency directly impacts campaign launch speed and capital turnover efficiency. In competitive markets, every day's delay may mean higher customer acquisition costs and lower competitive advantage.</p>
<p>Making invisible work visible, centralizing scattered information, clarifying ambiguous responsibilities—these are the three principles of refined operations Yiyu Digital Media distilled from serving numerous overseas teams. Systematic kanban boards are the concrete implementation of these three principles in the agency account setup scenario.</p>
